@lang en
IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `nii-il04-nouns`
IMPORT `nii-schedule-j-tables`
IMPORT `nii-schedule-j`

-- TESTS EXPECTED TO FAIL, BY AN EXACT COUNT: 15 of the 41 assertions below.
-- check.sh's `expected_failed` table names this module with 15, and encoding.json `expected_red`
-- says the same. (14 of 40 before the repair of 2026-10-07, which added family 3.) A run with any other number of failures here is red.
--
-- Two families, each a comparison the source (or the regulator) invites and the source does not
-- pass. They are findings, not bugs in the encoding: the items are encoded as printed, the totals
-- are encoded as printed, and they do not agree. Which is right is an open question for a domain
-- expert (NOTES.md, fork F4, and the open questions).
--
-- 1. Each table of Schedule J ends with a totals row ("סך הכל"). Read as a total, each cell is the
--    sum of the figures printed above it. All nine columns of all four readings of the schedule
--    are checked: 36 assertions, 10 expected to fail.
--      temporary version, as printed         column C above/employee (14.50 printed, items 14.39)
--                                            column D upper part      (7.00 printed, items 4.67)
--      temporary version, as read 2024-2027  column C above/employee (14.60 printed, items 14.49)
--                                            column D upper part      (7.00 printed, items 4.67)
--      permanent version, as printed         column C above/employee (14.50, items 14.39)
--                                            column C not above/employee (3.85, items 4.16)
--                                            column D upper part      (7.00, items 4.67)
--      permanent version, as read 2024-2027  column C above/employee (14.60, items 14.49)
--                                            column C not above/employee (3.95, items 4.26)
--                                            column D upper part      (7.00, items 4.67)
--    (Computed before this module was written, by summing the source's cells with Python's
--    Decimal; the figures above are that computation's output.)
--
-- 2. The National Insurance Institute's rate pages (fetched 2026-10-06; provenance in
--    nii-il04-published-figures.l4) print composite National Insurance rates for 2026 ABOVE the
--    reduced collection threshold that the items of the 2026 version do not reach: 4 assertions,
--    all 4 expected to fail.
--      employee, all branches: 14.6% (items 14.49)
--      employee's deduction, all deduction branches: 7% (items 4.67)
--      controlling shareholder (form 102 "column 2"): 19.34% with health insurance at 5.17%,
--        so 14.17% National Insurance (items, without unemployment and insolvency: 14.12)
--      controlling shareholder's deduction: 11.96% with health insurance at 5.17%, so 6.79%
--        (items, without unemployment: 4.46)
--    The same pages' composites UP TO the threshold, and for the self-employed and those who are
--    neither on both parts, ARE reached by the items; those assertions are in nii-il04-tests.l4.

§ `Expected red`

`all nine figure columns` MEANS
    LIST `column C, above the threshold, employee`, `column C, above the threshold, self-employed`
       , `column C, above the threshold, neither`, `column C, not above the threshold, employee`
       , `column C, not above the threshold, self-employed`, `column C, not above the threshold, neither`
       , `column D, upper part of the wage`, `column D, part of the wage not above the threshold`, `column E`

